{"page": 41, "text": "Chlor Alkali Products and Vinyls Three Months Ended September 30, 2026 Compared to Three Months Ended September 30, 2025 Chlor Alkali Products and Vinyls sales for the three months ended September 30, 2026 were $850.8 million compared to $924.0 million for the same period in 2025, a decrease of $73.2 million, or 8%. The sales decrease was due to lower sales volumes, primarily as a result of lower trading volumes associated with Blue Water Alliance, partially offset by higher pricing. Chlor Alkali Products and Vinyls segment income was $71.3 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $127.6 million for the same period in 2025, a decrease of $56.3 million. The decrease in segment income was primarily due to operating issues with the VCM plant at the Freeport, TX facility resulting in higher costs and reduced profit from lost sales ($32.1 million), a lower benefit primarily related to the 45V tax credit ($27.0 million), lower volumes ($7.5 million) and higher raw material costs ($4.8 million). These increases were partially offset by higher pricing ($8.0 million), primarily caustic soda and EDC, and lower operating costs ($7.1 million). Chlor Alkali Products and Vinyls segment results included depreciation and amortization expense of $91.1 million and $109.0 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively. Nine Months Ended September 30, 2026 Compared to Nine Months Ended September 30, 2025 Chlor Alkali Products and Vinyls sales for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 were $2,427.2 million compared to $2,828.0 million for the same period in 2025, a decrease of $400.8 million, or 14%. The sales decrease was due to lower sales volumes, primarily as a result of lower trading volumes associated with Blue Water Alliance, and lower pricing. Chlor Alkali Products and Vinyls segment income was $80.2 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $270.8 million for the same period in 2025, a decrease of $190.6 million. The decrease in segment income was primarily due to operating issues with the VCM plant at the Freeport, TX facility resulting in higher costs and reduced profit from lost sales ($72.2 million), lower volumes ($55.3 million), lower pricing ($42.0 million), a charge associated with legacy litigation matters ($36.1 million), higher raw material costs ($32.1 million), primarily natural gas and electrical power costs, and a lower benefit primarily related to the 45V tax credit ($12.1 million). These decreases were partially offset by lower operating costs ($59.2 million). Chlor Alkali Products and Vinyls segment results included depreciation and amortization expense of $282.4 million and $322.5 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively. 34 Table of Contents Epoxy Three Months Ended September 30, 2026 Compared to Three Months Ended September 30, 2025 Epoxy sales for the three months ended September 30, 2026 were $412.2 million compared to\n"}
{"page": 42, "text": "$349.6 million for the same period in 2025, an increase of $62.6 million, or 18%. The sales increase was due to higher product pricing ($35.3 million), higher volumes ($26.5 million) and a favorable effect of foreign currency translation ($0.8 million). Epoxy segment income was $0.6 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to segment loss of $(32.2) million for the same period in 2025. The increase in segment results of $32.8 million was due to higher product pricing ($35.3 million), lower operating costs ($20.0 million) and higher volumes ($15.8 million), partially offset by higher raw material costs ($38.3 million), primarily benzene and propylene. A significant percentage of our Euro denominated sales are from products manufactured within Europe. As a result, the impact of foreign currency translation on revenue is primarily offset by the impact of foreign currency translation on raw materials and manufacturing costs also denominated in Euros. Epoxy segment results included depreciation and amortization expense of $11.1 million and $13.2 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively. Nine Months Ended September 30, 2026 Compared to Nine Months Ended September 30, 2025 Epoxy sales for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 were $1,189.9 million compared to $1,012.5 million for the same period in 2025, an increase of $177.4 million, or 18%. The sales increase was due to higher volumes ($109.7 million), higher product pricing ($44.1 million) and a favorable effect of foreign currency translation ($23.6 million). Epoxy segment income was $13.7 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to segment loss of $(84.3) million for the same period in 2025. The increase in segment results of $98.0 million was due to lower operating costs ($59.5 million), higher product pricing ($44.1 million) and higher volumes ($27.5 million), partially offset by higher raw material costs ($33.1 million), primarily benzene and propylene. A significant percentage of our Euro denominated sales are from products manufactured within Europe. As a result, the impact of foreign currency translation on revenue is primarily offset by the impact of foreign currency translation on raw materials and manufacturing costs also denominated in Euros. Epoxy segment results included depreciation and amortization expense of $34.7 million and $39.1 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively. Winchester Three Months Ended September 30, 2026 Compared to Three Months Ended September 30, 2025 Winchester sales were $484.7 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $439.6 million for the same period in 2025, an increase of $45.1 million, or 10%. The sales increase was due to higher sales to commercial customers ($37.7 million), higher sales to military customers and military project revenue ($7.3 million) and higher sales to law enforcement agencies ($0.1 million).\n"}
